\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Universal Hurwitz coupling leaves the internal right algebra}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For the \vocabk{constant right actions}{fdip-00VK}, over
every commutative ring #{k} and every uniform period
#{N\ge1}, the geometric part of the universal commutant is
the scalar action. The full
\vocabk{universal right-action commutant}{fdip-00VK} is
exactly the internal right algebra
##{\{R_a^{\mathrm{int}}:a\in\mathcal O_k\}
       \cong\mathcal O_k^{\mathrm{op}}.}
This includes #{n=2} and #{n=4}. At the free background
all geometric right actions commute when #{N=1} or
#{N=2}; for #{N\ge3} the free geometric commutant is
already scalar.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{L_z} for left multiplication by a coefficient
#{z\in\mathcal O_k}. Geometric right actions commute with
#{e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot}, which is left Clifford multiplication.
Internal right actions commute with every #{L_z}.
Both families commute with every vertex gauge. The free
period statements are \ref{fdip-00OT}, with an unchanged
coefficient factor.}

\p{First suppose #{N=1}. Put the unit #{\qi} on direction
#{i} and identity links on the other directions. The
inverse-link formula gives #{T_i=-L_{\qi}} and
#{T_i^{-1}=L_{\qi}}. Thus
##{\extD_U=-(e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot)\otimes\bbI
              -(e_i\wedge-e_i\cdot)\otimes L_{\qi}.}
The first summand commutes with every geometric right
action. Since #{L_{\qi}} is invertible, the commutator
equation forces commutation with
#{e_i\wedge-e_i\cdot}. Repeat this for each direction.}

\p{The integral basis unit #{h} of \ref{fdip-00V1}
satisfies #{h^{-1}=1-h}. With only this link nontrivial,
the same transport formula gives
##{\extD_U=-(e_i\wedge-e_i\cdot)\otimes L_h
                 -(e_i\cdot)\otimes\bbI.}
The preceding constraint removes the first commutator,
so the action commutes with contraction. It then commutes
with exterior multiplication as well. This argument uses
the integral unit #{h}; it makes no division by #{2} in
#{k}.}

\p{For #{N=2}, fix a vertex #{x} and change only the
positive labeled link #{(x,i)} from #{1} to #{h}.
The incoming positive link #{(x+\varepsilon_i,i)}
is a different label and remains #{1}. The change in
the block carrying the cochain at #{x+\varepsilon_i}
to the value at #{x} is
##{-(e_i\wedge)\otimes L_h,}
because #{h^{-1}-1=-h}. Subtract the free and changed
commutator equations. Arbitrary values at this neighbor
and invertibility of #{L_h} force commutation with
#{e_i\wedge}. Commutation with the left Clifford sum
then gives commutation with #{e_i\cdot}.}

\p{If #{N\ge3}, the universal condition includes the free
background. The vertices #{x\pm\varepsilon_i} are then
distinct from one another and from #{x}. Prescribing a
cochain at one neighbor and zero at the others separates
the forward exterior multiplication and the backward
contraction. Their commutators must vanish separately,
as in the proof of \ref{fdip-00OT}.}

\p{In all three period cases, a surviving geometric right
action commutes with every exterior multiplication and
contraction. Their products span the full endomorphism
ring by \ref{fdip-0092}. Commutation with its matrix
units makes the action scalar on the monomial basis.
Its value on #{1} is #{c}, so #{c} is that scalar.
Conversely scalar actions commute with everything.}

\p{For a general element of #{\mathcal R}, the two
families commute with one another, so it has an expression
##{\sum_{a\in\{1,\qi,\qj,h\}}
       R_{c_a}^{\mathrm{geom}}R_a^{\mathrm{int}}.}
This expression is unique: applying it to
#{1\otimes1} in the two algebra factors gives
#{\sum_a c_a\otimes a}, and the displayed internal
elements are a basis. More generally the operators
#{R_a^{\mathrm{int}}} are linearly independent, since
evaluation at #{1} gives that basis. They remain
independent after an invertible left multiplication.
Consequently each of the preceding background
commutator equations imposes its geometric constraint
on every #{c_a} separately. Each #{c_a} is scalar,
leaving precisely an internal right multiplication.
Finally #{R_a^{\mathrm{int}}R_b^{\mathrm{int}}
=R_{ba}^{\mathrm{int}}}, which gives the opposite
algebra.}
}
